{"externally_confirmed_facts":["`javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec` is a `SecretKey` implementation that specifies a secret key using key material supplied as a byte array and an associated algorithm name. Its documentation says it is useful for raw secret keys representable as byte arrays with no key parameters. The two constructors accept `(byte[] key, String algorithm)` or `(byte[] key, int offset, int len, String algorithm)`. Thus, the reported `ARCFOUR` value refers to the constructors’ `algorithm` argument, rather than a cipher transformation or provider-selection argument. Source: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/crypto/spec/SecretKeySpec.html","The `SecretKeySpec` constructors document argument validation for null or empty key material, null algorithm names, and invalid offset/length bounds. They do not document an algorithm-policy allow-list or reject `ARCFOUR` specifically. The Javadoc directs callers to the Java Security Standard Algorithm Names specification for algorithm names. Java SE 25 lists `ARCFOUR` (RC4) as a standard `SecretKey` algorithm name. Therefore, the reported rejection of `ARCFOUR` is a rule/detector policy restriction, not a `SecretKeySpec` constructor restriction documented by the API. Sources: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/crypto/spec/SecretKeySpec.html ; https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/specs/security/standard-names.html","The `SecretKeySpec` Javadoc documents that constructors copy the supplied key material; `getEncoded()` returns the key material in a new byte array; and `getFormat()` returns `"RAW"`. It also expressly warns that constructors do not check whether the key material is valid for the declared algorithm. For algorithm-specific validation, its documented example is to use an algorithm-specific key-specification class such as `DESKeySpec`; the two-argument constructor says one should use that class when such checks are needed, and the offset/length constructor says one must use it. Source: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/crypto/spec/SecretKeySpec.html","The official `SecretKeySpec` documentation does not provide an `ARCFOUR` replacement recommendation, a general safe-algorithm selection policy, or a migration procedure for changing the declared algorithm. `KeyGenerator` is the Java API for symmetric-key generation; its Javadoc recommends explicitly initializing a generator rather than relying on provider defaults because defaults can vary between providers and future versions. This is general generation guidance and is not a documented `SecretKeySpec` migration path. Source: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/crypto/KeyGenerator.html","For compatibility, `SecretKeySpec` implements `Serializable`. The Java SE serialized-form documentation lists serialized `algorithm` (`String`) and `key` (`byte[]`) fields, with serialVersionUID `6577238317307289933L`. Separately, the API documents a raw (`"RAW"`) key encoding via `getEncoded()` and does not define an application wire protocol, an algorithm-negotiation format, a persisted-data conversion format, or versioning semantics for application data. Sources: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/crypto/spec/SecretKeySpec.html ; https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/serialized-form.html","`SecretKeySpec` is documented as available since Java 1.4. Java SE 25 lists `ARCFOUR` among standard names, but the standard-names specification says implementations can support additional algorithms and directs users to implementation/release documentation for provider support; the Java SE mandatory-support tables do not make all standard names mandatory. Consequently, provider and target-runtime support for a changed algorithm must be established from the relevant implementation documentation, not from `SecretKeySpec` alone. Sources: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/crypto/spec/SecretKeySpec.html ; https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/specs/security/standard-names.html","The supplied primary rule source declares the governed type as `javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec` and its exact matching declaration is:
```
SPEC javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec
OBJECTS
	java.lang.String keyAlgorithm;
	byte[] keyMaterial;
	int len;
	int offset;

EVENTS
	c1: SecretKeySpec(keyMaterial, keyAlgorithm);
	c2: SecretKeySpec(keyMaterial, offset, len, keyAlgorithm);
	Con := c1 | c2;
```
